  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.2 x^2 + 0.6 z^2) / 2, drawn at half its value as height. x is the steep direction, so with no noise x shrinks by a factor of (1 - 2.2 × rate) each step. Swings grow once the rate is above 2/2.2 ≈ 0.91.",
    "Each step moves the ball by -rate × (slope + noise). The noise has standard deviation sqrt((64-b)/(63b)) on each axis: 1 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. It is random and centred on zero, so a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope estimate), drawn at 3 times their true length, one scale for both, starting at the ball's edge. Mini-batch step = the move this batch makes. Full-data step = against the true slope. Arrows are hidden once a run ends.",
    "Status: paused, running, finished (40 steps done) or diverged (loss above 4.4, or the ball leaves the drawn bowl). The trail holds one point per step, at most 40, and so does the ghost.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence. A new run carries on with the next noise values, so its noise is different."
  ]
}